461-10=451
To find out how many shelves Oliver will need, first he has to divide 451 by 4. 451/4 = 112.5
He will have .5 of a shelf left over. Since Oliver can only use a shelf in total , he will need to round up to the nearest whole number . This means he will need 113  shelves to put all his books away. 
The final answer is: \boxed{113} 
Here's how you can create the math problem and the solution in LaTeX. I used a quick google search to figure out how to use LaTeX.
\documentclass{article}
\begin{document}
\section{Solution}
Oliver is at the library helping put away books. There 
are, 46 book to put away total but a librarian takes 10 of them! and leaves Oliver with the; rest. If he can fit 4 books on a shelf, how many shelves will he need? 
To find out how many shelves Oliver will need, first he has to divide 451 by 4. \begin{align*}
451\div4=112.5
\end{align*}
He will have .5 of a shelf left over. Since Oliver can only use a shelf in total , he will need to \emph{round up} to the nearest whole number. This means he will need  $112+\left\lfloor\frac{.5}{1}\right\rfloor=\boxed{113}$  shelves to put all his books away. 
\end{document} 
Created by L3GANANN (just kidding its me). I used a LaTeX compiler to quickly type up this response.  LaTex is great for making cool math problems and answers, and is
pretty cool for typing up anything when you want clean clean fonts and spacing.  You can copy the above code to a text file, save it as .tex and use a latex compiler to view the solution typeset and formatted with pretty fonts and margins. If you want to do the same, I recommend download Miktex on windows if you are on a windows machine, or TexShop if you are on a mac. It seems that I am correct on my solution to the original question posed by the math question, and I welcome any corrections that you may want to provide.
